1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,441
Before we start, Spiros and Hiroko, thanks for coming in today to talk about your recent study experiences

2
00:00:06,441 --> 00:00:12,460
and congratulations to you both in doing so well in your first semester exams!

3
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:17,341
I'd like to discuss with you the value of the English for Academic Purposes

4
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:21,620
course you did here last year before starting your university course.

5
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:27,261
Spiros, if I could start with you, what parts of the progamme have now

6
00:00:27,261 --> 00:00:29,670
proved to be particularly valuable to you?

7
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:34,450
I think that having to do a seminar presentation really helped me.

8
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:39,211
For example, a couple of weeks ago in our marketing subject,

9
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:43,310
when it was my turn to give a presentation I felt quite confident.

10
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:48,455
Of course, I was still nervous but because I had done one before, I

11
00:00:48,455 --> 00:00:49,500
knew what to expect.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,100
Also, I know I was well-prepared and I had practised my timing.

13
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:57,896
In fact, I think that in relation to some of the other people in

14
00:00:57,896 --> 00:01:02,837
my group, I did quite a good job because my overall style was quite

15
00:01:02,837 --> 00:01:03,460
professional.

16
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:05,350
What about you, Hiroko?

17
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:05,972
Mmm,

18
00:01:05,972 --> 00:01:07,235
that's interesting.

19
00:01:07,235 --> 00:01:12,387
In my group, I was really surprised by the way the students did their

20
00:01:12,387 --> 00:01:16,940
presentations they just read their notes aloud!Can you believe that?

21
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:22,130
They didn't worry about their presentation style or keeping eye contact with their audience -

22
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:27,319
and I remember that these things were really stressed to us in the course

23
00:01:27,319 --> 00:01:27,690
here.

24
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:31,950
So, how did you approach your presentation, Hiroko?

25
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:36,330
Well, to speak frankly, I read my notes too!

26
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:40,458
At the time, it was a relief to do it this way,

27
00:01:40,458 --> 00:01:45,618
but actually when I had finished, I didn't feel any real sense of satisfaction.I

28
00:01:45,618 --> 00:01:48,370
didn't feel positive about the experience at all.

29
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:49,425
That's a pity.

30
00:01:49,425 --> 00:01:53,646
You know, although I was pleased with my presentation, I am not

31
00:01:53,646 --> 00:01:58,571
so pleased with my actual performance right now in the tutorials-during the whole semester

32
00:01:58,571 --> 00:02:01,385
I've not said anything in our tutorial discussions.

33
00:02:01,385 --> 00:02:02,440
Not a word.

34
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:03,345
Really, Spiros?

35
00:02:03,345 --> 00:02:04,251
Why's that?

36
00:02:04,251 --> 00:02:07,420
Do the other students talk too much?

37
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:08,450
It's partly that,

38
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:12,570
but it's mostly because I have had no confidence to speak out.

39
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:16,716
Their style of speaking is so different - It's not the style we were

40
00:02:16,716 --> 00:02:18,310
used to during the course.

41
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:23,742
They use so many colloquialisms, they're not very polite and sometimes there seems to

42
00:02:23,742 --> 00:02:26,070
be no order in their discussion.

43
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:28,679
Also, they are very familiar with each other,

44
00:02:28,679 --> 00:02:33,244
so because they know each other's habits, they can let each other into the

45
00:02:33,244 --> 00:02:33,570
discussion.

46
00:02:33,570 --> 00:02:37,370
You're right, Spiros, I've experienced that too.

47
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:41,850
For most of this semester, I've said absolutely nothing in tutorials.

48
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:46,551
But recently, I've been trying to speak up more and I jump in,

49
00:02:46,551 --> 00:02:52,337
and I've noticed an interesting thing, I've noticed that if they thought my point was interesting

50
00:02:52,337 --> 00:02:56,677
or new, then the next time they actually asked for my opinion,

51
00:02:56,677 --> 00:03:01,740
and then it was much easier for me to be part of the discussion.

52
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:02,714
That's great, Hiroko!

53
00:03:02,714 --> 00:03:06,936
I hope that happens for me next Semester - I'll have to work hard

54
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:08,560
to find some interesting points.

55
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:11,110
What helped you to find these ideas?

56
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:15,250
I think that one thing that helped me with this was the reading.

57
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:19,442
I've had to do so much reading this semester just to help me make

58
00:03:19,442 --> 00:03:20,640
sense of the lectures.

59
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:24,810
At first I couldn't understand what the lecturers were talking about,

60
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:28,600
so I had to turn to the books and journals.

61
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:34,181
Every night I read for hours, using the lists of references that were given,

62
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:36,572
and I made pages of notes.

63
00:03:36,572 --> 00:03:40,160
At breakfast, I read and read my notes again.

64
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:44,540
This habit has helped me to follow the ideas in the lectures,

65
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:48,920
and it's also given me some ideas to use in the tutorials.

66
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:53,680
But I did so much reading anyway-I don't think there's any time left over

67
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:54,700
for anything extra.

68
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:59,131
My reading speed is still quite slow, though I'm much better at dealing with

69
00:03:59,131 --> 00:04:01,030
vocabulary than I used to be.

70
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:05,673
What else do you think we could add to the course program to help

71
00:04:05,673 --> 00:04:07,000
with this reading problem?

72
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,220
There's not really anything because it's my problem.

73
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:14,300
I remember we were given long articles to read.

74
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:18,576
We didn't like that but now I realise that reading those long articles was

75
00:04:18,576 --> 00:04:21,630
good preparation for the things I need to read now.

76
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:25,361
Also, in class we regularly had speed-reading tasks to do,

77
00:04:25,361 --> 00:04:28,719
and we kept a record of our reading speed,

78
00:04:28,719 --> 00:04:32,450
so the teachers were encouraging us to work on that.

79
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:33,669
That's true Spiros,

80
00:04:33,669 --> 00:04:36,920
but what we read could have been different.

81
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:43,656
Sometimes in the English class I felt frustrated when I had to read articles about the environment

82
00:04:43,656 --> 00:04:45,241
or health or education,

83
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:48,806
because I wanted to concentrate on my own field,

84
00:04:48,806 --> 00:04:51,580
but we didn't read anything about engineering.

85
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:56,510
So, I think I wasted some time learning vocabulary I didn't need.

86
00:04:56,510 --> 00:05:01,550
But surely the strategies you were taught for dealing with that vocabulary were helpful.

87
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:01,947
Yes,

88
00:05:01,947 --> 00:05:07,506
but psychologically speaking, I would have felt much better working on reading from my

89
00:05:07,506 --> 00:05:08,300
own field.

90
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,430
What do you think Spiros?

91
00:05:10,430 --> 00:05:11,026
I agree;

92
00:05:11,026 --> 00:05:15,200
that would have helped my confidence too and I would have been more motivated.

93
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:19,198
It was good though that we could work on our own topics when we

94
00:05:19,198 --> 00:05:20,340
wrote the research assignments.

95
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:24,000
Okay, let's move on to writing now…
